iate antibiotic therapy  and to enable antibiotic stewardship we  need on one hand solutions that diagnose  or predict resistance to enable  targeted therapy with appropriate  antibiotics as well as solutions that  enable the global surveillance for amr  to allow us to detect outbreaks to  counter them with infection prevention  measures and to empirically inform  first-line antibiotic selection  let's take a look at the technologies  that are in the clinic today  the standard of care in infectious  disease diagnosis are bacterial cultures  which are routinely used for the  identification of infective agents and  the  determination of suitable antibiotic  treatment options  whenever a culture is positive meaning  if if a pathogen  if pathogen growth is present then  antibiotic susceptibility testing  also referred to ast is performed in  order to determine which antibiotics are  effective and which will likely be met  with resistance  while these culture-based methods are  being considered the gold standard they  are often  labor intensive and may miss difficult  to culture pathogens  turnaround time ranges from one to three  days  or more which is long considering that  patients with acute infections  require urgent care often within hours  this lack of sensitivity and speed is  addressed by modern molecular tests  relying on pcr  leading pcr panels such as the tests  available on the curator's universe  system  achieve very high sensitivities and can  return results in a matter of hours  even pushing the limit below one hour  however these systems can only screen  for up to a few hundred biomarkers at  best  this means each assay which has to be  individually developed validated and  potentially approved  can cover a limited number of pathogens  and for each pathogen  only a limited number of genetic markers  for amr  given that today the number of genetic  markers for amr is measured in the  thousands  the question has to be is there a system  that could simultaneously and  comprehensively screen for any pathogen  and all known genetic markers for  antibiotic resistance  and what if the system would not only  report the detected amar markers  but also emulate the advantages of  culture by predicting whether  detective pathogens are susceptible or  resistant to antibiotic therapy  this is where next generation sequencing  or ngs enters the picture  ngs is increasingly adopted by clinical  labs and its utility  for example for microbial typing and  epidemiology is already undisputed  it is the first technology that thanks  to an enormous sampling depth is not  only  very sensitive but also captures the  entire genome  allowing for an exhaustive  characterization of genetic amar markers  in a single test  moreover such comprehensive genomic  information  provides a chance to use ai and  predictive modeling  to emulate culture-based ast and predict  which antibiotics represent suitable  treatment options  with sequencing costs and turnaround  times rapidly decreasing  trying to overcome the shortcomings in  the current standard of care through  sequencing based predictive  antimicrobial susceptibility testing  has therefore firmly emerged as a focus  for aerys genetics  allow me to share with you some of the  key challenges but also the milestones  we've met on our mission to  revolutionize infectious disease testing  next  once our objective had been set on  enabling accurate and fast detection of  pathogens and the prediction of amr  we started to address potential  roadblocks very quickly databases for  amr markers  emerged as a critical technological  component  when we evaluated several public america  databases for their suitability  for predictive ast we recognized a  number of shortcomings  here two examples the annotation of  amr markers at the level of antibiotic  classes  was two cores for machine learning  considering that different antibiotic  compounds  within the same class are affected  differently by any given amr marker  for example because of different  enzymatic affinities  therefore raising the resolution of  marker annotation from antibiotic class  to the level of antibiotic compound  substantially improved predictive  modeling  in addition simple rule-based models for  individual markers tended to over  generalize  because multiple genetic factors can for  example synergistically or  antagonistically contribute to the mr  phenotype  we needed to adjust for biology and  allow for polyvary predictive models  based on these and other findings we  decided to develop airsdb  a comprehensive amr knowledge base  designed from ground up to  enable predictive ast rs2b today is a  globally leading database of curated amr  markers and information about resistance  derived from genotypic and phenotypic  data  what sets arsdb apart from other  databases is not only comprehensiveness  but that it also harbors a continuously  growing collection of clinical pathogen  genomes and associated validated ast  measurements  that determine how any given pathogen in  this database has responded  phenotypically  in the lab when exposed to a wide range  of antibiotics  this additional data layer offers two  unique benefits  these genotype phenotype data  associations  can be used for machine learning more  specifically to train predictive models  that not only discover novel biomarkers  for amr  but that can predict how pathogens  respond to therapy based on genomic data  a process we refer to as predictive ast  or pasd  at aeros genetics currently the models  to predict antibiotic susceptibility are  trained as stacked ensemble models  on ast results and on a variety of  genetic markers including k-mers  gene and protein annotations and others  all collected within the rsdb  this model-based approach can then be  complemented by a rule-based approach  taking into account the interplay  between  multiple resistance mechanisms and  antibiotic compounds  as the figure in the middle illustrates  we not only rely on the presence  absence of certain resistance genes but  we also consider  changes in non-coding regions of the  genome as well as novel markers  that our predictive algorithms have  discovered  allow me to introduce over the next  couple of slides how we use rsdb  and predictive modeling to enable a  number of sequencing based solutions for  amr and pathogen detection  and how we validate the performance of  these solutions  the resolution spectrum today includes a  number of next generation sequencing  services  and software central to our offering is  and remains  the proprietary rsdb this database  database powers  both rsiss our service for bacterial  isolate sequencing  as well as the newly introduced areas  upa  arres upa is not the focus of this  presentation today but i will say that  it is a target enrichment ngs panel  that comprehensively covers pathogens as  well as thousands of genetic markers  we have selected from the rsdb this  essay is currently  used in collaboration with five early  access customers in the public health  space in five different countries  and finally the iris cloud on the right  side is a cloud-based user-friendly  interpretation and reporting software  which enables our own ngs service lab  and is available to other ngs labs  we demonstrated in several studies the  high categorical  categorical agreement between standard  of care results  with the resistance phenotypes we  predicted based on ngs data using  predictive ast  these studies include for instance the  first into our knowledge only  multi-center evaluation  that is successfully demonstrating the  feasibility and performance  of predictive ast for 13 pathogens  causing lower respiratory tract  infections  some of the study results are shown here  and i apologize for the data-rich nature  of this slide  the matrix shows along the horizontal  axis the samples for the various  pathogens  reported by the standard of care the  vertical axis  lists the different antibody compounds  by compound class  that were tested for the intersections  or the fields in this table  show the categorical agreement in  percent between aries predictive ast  and culture if you now exclude  all pathogen drug combinations for which  pathogens are  intrinsically resistant those are the  ones here in blue  and those in gray for which the standard  of care method did not detect any  resistance  then the remaining white fields  oops sorry  then the remaining white fields on the  table are the areas in which  a direct comparison of rspasd and  culture based ast is possible  as the green fields indicate or be able  to demonstrate high concordance  reaching for most of the pathogen drug  combinations values significantly above  the regulatory threshold for ast devices  of greater than 90  and achieving up to 100 studies like  these are not only important for  clinical validation purposes  we also embrace them as they give our  data science teams clear indicators  on which passage and drug combinations  to focus our efforts on for predictive  model development  and for database curation  encouraged by these findings and to help  translate this technology into  applications  we have more recently validated the  entire end-to-end workflow  from sequencing of isolates on illumina  the de facto standard ngs platform  all the way through the data  interpretation via irsdb  and rs cloud using our ai-powered  bioinformatics pipeline  the workflow performance was very robust  in terms of  repeatability reproducibility  sensitivity and specificity  reaching consistently 100 for pathogen  identification  multi-local sequence typing and  genotyping and values in the high 90s  for amr market detection we  we did actually apply clear standards  for to the validation process and we  published both performance as well  as validation protocols in order to make  iris technology portable to other ngs  laboratories  so in conclusion the use of rsdb  which everyone